@model RulesIndexViewModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Rules";
}

<h1 class="h3 mb-3">Rules</h1>

<p class="text-muted">
    These rules decide how a break is read from the punches of a device that sends no break type, and which findings (a long, late or early
    break, a shift worked with no break, a no-show) become an exception for a person to settle. They apply to everyone on a schedule, or to the
    whole company. This is the one place to change them. Until a set is published, the starting rules apply. A change is made on a draft and
    published as a new version; a published set is never edited. A rule can never invent a punch.
</p>

<details class="card card-body mb-3">
    <summary class="fw-semibold">How the attendance policy and these rules work together</summary>
    <ul class="small mt-2 mb-0">
        <li>
            <strong>The policy holds the numbers that are not about breaks or exceptions</strong> (<a asp-controller="Policies" asp-action="Index">Policies</a>): rounding, late and early-departure grace,
            the overtime threshold, how long a missing check-out waits, and who approves a correction.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The rules decide everything about breaks and their exceptions</strong>: how short a break may be before it counts as work (a toilet visit), what counts as a finding
            (how many minutes over the scheduled break is a long break, how many minutes from its scheduled start is a late or early one, how long after the shift ends a shift with no
            punch is a no-show), whether the finding becomes an exception for a person to settle, and whether a gap between a check-out and the next check-in is read as the break.
            These numbers are conditions of the rules, so change them on the rule.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Which rules apply to an employee:</strong> if the employee's schedule has a policy of its own, that schedule's rules; otherwise the company's.
            Rules published for the company do not reach a schedule that has its own policy: give that schedule its own set.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>For a finding the first enabled rule whose conditions all hold decides</strong>; a rule that suppresses comes before the one it overrides. A finding no rule matches is not raised, so a published
            set that leaves a finding out switches it off.
        </li>
        <li>
            A rule that is <strong>off</strong> does not raise an exception, but its conditions still say when the finding is noted on the day (a long break over 5 minutes, say). Delete the rule
            and the finding is not noted at all.
        </li>
        <li>
            A rule can never invent a punch. The checks under <em>Always applied</em> below are not rules: they are about whether the hours of a day can be calculated at all, so they cannot be
            switched off or made less strict.
        </li>
        <li>A change applies to days calculated from then on; the nightly look-back calculates again the days last checked with another set.</li>
    </ul>
</details>

<partial name="_StatusMessage" />

<ul class="nav nav-pills mb-3">
    @foreach (var scope in Model.Scopes)
    {
        <li class="nav-item">
            <a class="nav-link @(scope.TimeScheduleId == Model.ScheduleId ? "active" : string.Empty)" asp-action="Index" asp-route-scheduleId="@scope.TimeScheduleId">
                @scope.Title
                @if (scope.HasDraft)
                {
                    <span class="badge text-bg-warning ms-1">draft</span>
                }
            </a>
        </li>
    }
</ul>

<div class="card mb-4">
    <div class="card-header d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center">
        <span>
            <strong>@Model.Current.ScopeTitle</strong>:
            @if (Model.Current.Status == "Policy")
            {
                <span class="text-muted">the starting rules (nothing published yet)</span>
            }
            else
            {
                <span class="text-muted">published version @Model.Current.Version@(Model.Current.PublishedAt is { } at ? " on " + at.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm") : string.Empty)</span>
            }
        </span>
        <form method="post" asp-action="StartDraft" asp-route-scheduleId="@Model.ScheduleId">
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-sm btn-primary">@(Model.HasDraft ? "Continue the draft" : "Change these rules")</button>
        </form>
    </div>
    @if (Model.Current.Rules.Count == 0)
    {
        <partial name="_RulesTable" model="Model.Current" />
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="card-body border-bottom small text-muted">
            @if (Model.HasDraft)
            {
                <span>A draft of these rules is open, so a rule can't be changed one by one. Continue the draft or throw it away first.</span>
            }
            else
            {
                <span>
                    Each button changes that one rule and publishes the rules at once as a new version; the version in force stays in the history.
                    @if (Model.Current.Status == "Policy")
                    {
                        <span>The first change publishes the starting rules, with your change, as the first version.</span>
                    }
                </span>
            }
        </div>
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table align-middle mb-0">
                <thead class="table-light">
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">#</th>
                        <th scope="col">Rule</th>
                        <th scope="col">Status</th>
                        <th scope="col" class="text-end">Manage</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @for (var i = 0; i < Model.Current.Rules.Count; i++)
                    {
                        var rule = Model.Current.Rules[i];
                        <tr>
                            <td>@(i + 1)</td>
                            <td>
                                <div class="fw-semibold">@rule.Name</div>
                                <div class="@(rule.IsEnabled ? string.Empty : "text-muted text-decoration-line-through")">@rule.Sentence</div>
                            </td>
                            <td><span class="badge text-bg-@(rule.IsEnabled ? "success" : "secondary")">@(rule.IsEnabled ? "On" : "Off")</span></td>
                            <td class="text-end text-nowrap">
                                <form method="post" asp-action="Manage" asp-route-scheduleId="@Model.ScheduleId" class="d-inline">
                                    <input type="hidden" name="version" value="@Model.Current.Version" />
                                    <input type="hidden" name="position" value="@i" />
                                    <button type="submit" name="operation" value="MoveUp" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary" title="Look at this rule earlier" disabled="@(Model.HasDraft || i == 0)">↑</button>
                                    <button type="submit" name="operation" value="MoveDown" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary" title="Look at this rule later" disabled="@(Model.HasDraft || i == Model.Current.Rules.Count - 1)">↓</button>
                                    <a class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary @(Model.HasDraft ? "disabled" : string.Empty)" asp-action="EditRule" asp-route-scheduleId="@Model.ScheduleId" asp-route-position="@i" asp-route-version="@Model.Current.Version">Modify</a>
                                    <button type="submit" name="operation" value="@(rule.IsEnabled ? "Disable" : "Enable")" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-warning" disabled="@Model.HasDraft">@(rule.IsEnabled ? "Disable" : "Enable")</button>
                                    <button type="submit" name="operation" value="Delete" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-danger" disabled="@Model.HasDraft" onclick="return confirm('Delete this rule? The rules are published at once without it.');">Delete</button>
                                </form>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    }
</div>

<h2 class="h5">Always applied</h2>
<div class="card mb-4">
    <div class="card-body border-bottom small text-muted">
        These raise an exception whatever the rules say, because without the punch the hours of the day cannot be calculated. They are shown here so the whole list is in one place.
    </div>
    <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
        <li class="list-group-item">
            <div class="fw-semibold">A check-in is missing</div>
            <div>When there are punches for a shift but no check-in, raise an exception for a missing check-in. A person supplies the punch; it is never invented.</div>
        </li>
        <li class="list-group-item">
            <div class="fw-semibold">A check-out is missing</div>
            <div>
                When the shift is over and the policy's wait for a missing check-out has passed, and the person is still checked in, raise an exception for a missing check-out.
                (The wait is on the <a asp-controller="Policies" asp-action="Index">policy</a>.)
            </div>
        </li>
        <li class="list-group-item">
            <div class="fw-semibold">A break punch is missing</div>
            <div>When a break-out has no break-in, or a break-in has no break-out, on a shift with a break, raise an exception for the missing break punch.</div>
        </li>
        <li class="list-group-item">
            <div class="fw-semibold">The punches are out of order</div>
            <div>
                The expected order is check-in, break-out, break-in, check-out. A check-in after a check-in, a break during no work, a check-out before a check-in and the like raise an
                exception for an invalid sequence, which the person settles by changing the type of a punch.
            </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>

<h2 class="h5">Versions</h2>
<div class="card">
    @if (Model.History.Count == 0)
    {
        <div class="card-body text-muted">No set has been published for this scope yet.</div>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table align-middle mb-0">
                <thead class="table-light">
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Version</th>
                        <th scope="col">Status</th>
                        <th scope="col">Published</th>
                        <th scope="col">By</th>
                        <th scope="col">Rules</th>
                        <th scope="col">Notes</th>
                        <th scope="col"><span class="visually-hidden">Actions</span></th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var set in Model.History)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td>@set.Version</td>
                            <td><span class="badge text-bg-@(set.Status == "Published" ? "success" : "secondary")">@set.Status</span></td>
                            <td class="text-nowrap">@(set.PublishedAt?.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm"))</td>
                            <td>@(set.PublishedByName ?? "—")</td>
                            <td>@set.RuleCount</td>
                            <td>@set.Notes</td>
                            <td class="text-end"><a class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary" asp-action="Version" asp-route-id="@set.Id">View</a></td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    }
</div>
